WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 106.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.220
Today is June 19th, 2026.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.080
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.740
A distant exoplanet once nicknamed the Pink Planet

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.260
turns out to host salty clouds detectable only through infrared observations

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.580
that pierce its thick atmosphere.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.160
Observations with the James Webb Space Telescope

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.680
reveal salt-bearing clouds in the atmosphere of a planet previously called the Pink Planet.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.780
The clouds form at temperatures low enough for ammonium hydro-sulfide and other salts to

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.160
condense. Spectral features appear only in transmission data taken during transit,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.160
confirming their presence high in the atmosphere.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.120
The detection adds a new class of condensates to models of cold giant planet chemistry.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.000
Follow-up observations will map cloud distribution across the terminator.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.560
From one remote atmospheric surprise to a mission that will study how our own atmosphere

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:06.320
couples to space weather. NASA has selected the dynamic atmosphere ionosphere explorer mission

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:13.440
concept to move into phase B development. Daphne will measure how solar activity and atmospheric

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.780
dynamics couple to shape the near-earth space environment. Data from the mission are expected

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.100
to improve forecasts that protect GPS signals and satellites in low-earth orbit.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.780
Instruments will target energy transfer between the lower atmosphere and the ionosphere.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.480
The selection follows a competitive review of proposals addressing space-weather impacts

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.900
on technology. While NASA prepares an Earth-orbiting observatory, a university team is sending

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:49.140
a probe to meet an asteroid already on its way here. A spacecraft led by Tsinghua University

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:55.360
will rendezvous with asteroid a poffess during its 2029 Earth encounter. The mission joins

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.060
planned efforts by other space agencies to study the 340 meter object at close range.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:07.240
Scientists aim to measure surface composition and any structural changes induced by tidal forces.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.580
The university developed probe will carry remote sensing instruments tailored for the brief fly-by window.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.640
Launch timing remains under study to meet the 2029 arrival. On a different note,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.220
spectral data from NASA's Lucy spacecraft show evidence of liquid water alteration on asteroid

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:33.040
Donald Johansson. The four-kilometer body formed farther from the Sun before migrating inward.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.000
Carbonate and phylo-silicate signatures indicate aqueous processing occurred early in its history.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.560
The findings help refine models of how water and organics were distributed in the inner

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:52.060
main belt. Additional Lucy encounters will test whether similar alteration appears on other targets.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:58.920
Southwest Research Institute Scientists report that asteroid Donald Johansson rotates about

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.320
two axes simultaneously. The body completes one end-over-end rotation every 10.5

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:13.000
Earth days, while its spin axis wobbles on a 26.5-day cycle. Light curve data from the Lucy spacecraft

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.800
fly-by provided the measurements. The complex motion likely results from the asteroid's irregular

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:24.980
shape and internal mass distribution. Future shape models will refine predictions of its long-term

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:31.360
rotational evolution. Mu technologies has acquired an aircraft to begin selling reduced gravity

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:37.440
flights to researchers and private customers. The move comes as NASA evaluates options for

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:44.180
replacing its own microgravity aircraft. Parabolic arcs will provide 22nd periods of weightlessness

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:50.340
for experiments in fluid dynamics and material science. The company plans initial operations

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.800
from European airports. Demand is driven by growing interest in short-duration microgravity access

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.700
outside government programs. The European Space Agency has activated the E5A quasi-pilot component

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.340
across 12 Galileo satellites. This new signal element reduces acquisition time and power draw

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.780
for receivers in internet of things networks and smart city sensors. Engineers designed it specifically

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:23.600
for mass market devices that cannot sustain continuous high-power tracking. The change expands

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:31.340
Galileo's reach into applications previously limited to stronger GNSS signals. Future firmware

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:37.220
updates on additional satellites will extend the capability further. A feral manganese crust

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:44.680
dredge from the Pacific in 1976 holds a few hundred atoms of plutonium isotopes produced in a neutron

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:50.760
star merger. The material settled onto Earth more than 100 million years after the event,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:58.140
then was incorporated into the growing nodule. Isotopic ratios match predictions for our process

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:05.540
nucleosynthesis in kilonave. The find provides a rare terrestrial sample of debris from such mergers.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.160
Further searches in other ocean cores may locate additional signatures.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:16.880
New analysis from Los Alamos National Laboratory supports a view that long-duration gamma

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.960
ray bursts arise from the collapse of massive stars rather than neutron star mergers.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.460
The team examined burst durations, host galaxy metallocities, and afterglow properties across

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:35.000
dozens of events. Results show consistency with the collapse our model in which a rapidly spinning

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.900
black hole powers relativistic jets. The findings narrow the range of progenitor scenarios

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.380
still under debate. Additional multi messenger data will test the conclusion further.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.540
A new study traces concentrations of gold in western australian rocks to a large meteorite

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:59.440
strike more than two billion years ago. The impact melted crustal material and allowed gold to

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:05.580
concentrate an impact related hydrothermal systems. Geochemical mapping shows the gold signature

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:11.500
aligns with the proposed crater location. The work adds to evidence that extraterrestrial collisions

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.420
have influenced or deposits on earth. Further dating of impact glasses will tighten the timeline.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.400
Astronomers have associated a high-energy neutrino event with a distant galaxy nicknamed the

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:29.820
shadow blaster. The galaxy's intense star formation produces cosmic rays whose interactions generate

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:35.800
neutrinos. If confirmed, this would mark the first direct link between an individual dusty

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:43.160
starburst galaxy and a neutrino detection. Ice cube observatory data, combined with multi-wave

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:49.260
length observations, narrowed the source position. Additional events will test whether such galaxies

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:55.160
form a significant neutrino population. Researchers suggest that instruments detecting energetic

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:00.980
neutral atoms could reveal the structure of Uranus' unusual magnetic field during a future mission.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:07.240
The atoms form when charged particles in the magnetosphere capture electrons from neutral gas

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:13.420
and travel in straight lines to a spacecraft detector. This technique has mapped magnet spheres

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:19.960
at earth and Saturn, and could clarify how Uranus' tilted offset field channels plasma.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:25.520
Proposed payloads would combine these measurements with plasma and magnetic field sensors.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.520
The approach offers remote sensing of regions difficult to reach within situ instruments alone.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:37.800
Data from the Alpha Magnetic Spectrometer on the International Space Station reveal four distinct

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:44.320
groups of cosmic rays spanning 20 elements. The population's differ in spectral shape and

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:51.080
elemental ratios, challenging current propagation models. One group shows enhanced secondary

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:57.560
to primary ratios consistent with extended confinement in the galaxy. The findings come from more

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:03.140
than a decade of continuous measurements above the atmosphere. Upcoming instrument upgrades aim

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:09.300
to extend the energy reach. Fermi large area telescope observations indicate two supernova

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:15.620
remnants may have originated from stars that once orbited each other. The first explosion ejected

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:21.660
its companion, which later exploded after traveling thousands of years. Spatial and velocity

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:28.380
data align with this binary disruption scenario. The remnants lie in a region where such pairings

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.300
are statistically rare. Continued gamma ray monitoring will search for similar sibling systems.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:39.080
The curiosity rover is traversing layered outcrops on mount sharp,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:45.520
the display contrasting textures and colors. Each band records different depositional or

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.880
diagenetic conditions from ancient gale crater lake environments. Scientists are mapping grain

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:56.680
size, mineralogy, and fracture patterns to reconstruct changing lake chemistry.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:02.680
The current campaign focuses on a series of finely laminated units at the base of one prominent

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:09.700
ridge. Data will feed into broader reconstructions of Mars early climate. If the Sun were the size

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:15.960
of a basketball, the nearest neutron star merger would have occurred roughly 3000 kilometers away.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:23.060
Yet its ejected material still found its way into a rock on our ocean floor. When two neutron stars

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:30.280
collide, they fling out neutron rich matter at 10 to 30% the speed of light. Rapid neutron capture

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:37.060
builds elements heavier than iron within seconds. That debris expands into a vast radioactive cloud

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:42.560
whose slowest moving atoms can drift for tens of millions of years before encountering a molecular

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:48.680
cloud that collapses into new stars and planets. In the case recorded in the Pacific

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.760
nodule, a few hundred plutonium atoms arrived after traveling across the Milky Way and settling

00:09:54.760 --> 00:10:00.860
through 100 million years of Earth's geological cycling. The lingering question is whether every

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:07.240
planetary system receives a comparable sprinkling of these exotic nuclei or whether our sample is

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:13.620
unusually well preserved. Next time, keep an eye on further analysis of the Donald Johansson fly

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:20.420
by data as Lucy continues its tour of the main belt. That covers today's space and science news.

